L286: ## 5.6. Replacing the standard import systemcite87†¶ L287: 
L288: The most reliable mechanism for replacing the entire import system is to delete the default contents of cite10†`sys.meta_path` , replacing them entirely with a custom meta path hook.
L289: If it is acceptable to only alter the behaviour of import statements without affecting other APIs that access the import system, then replacing the builtin cite5†`__import__()` function may be sufficient. This technique may also be employed at the module level to only alter the behaviour of import statements within that module.
L290: To selectively prevent the import of some modules from a hook early on the meta path (rather than disabling the standard import system entirely), it is sufficient to raise cite8†`ModuleNotFoundError` directly from cite36†`find_spec()` instead of returning `None`. The latter indicates that the meta path search should continue, while raising an exception terminates it immediately.
L291: ## 5.7. Package Relative Importscite88†¶ L292: Relative imports use leading dots. A single leading dot indicates a relative import, starting with the current package. Two or more leading dots indicate a relative import to the parent(s) of the current package, one level per dot after the first. For example, given the following package layout:
L293: 
L294:     package/
L295:         __init__.py
L296:         subpackage1/
L297:             __init__.py
L298:             moduleX.py
L299:             moduleY.py
L300:         subpackage2/
L301:             __init__.py
L302:             moduleZ.py
L303:         moduleA.py
L304: In either `subpackage1/moduleX.py` or `subpackage1/__init__.py`, the following are valid relative imports:
L305: 
L306:     from .moduleY import spam
L307:     from .moduleY import spam as ham
L308:     from . import moduleY
L309:     from ..subpackage1 import moduleY
L310:     from ..subpackage2.moduleZ import eggs
L311:     from ..moduleA import foo
L312: Absolute imports may use either the `import <>` or `from <> import <>` syntax, but relative imports may only use the second form; the reason for this is that:
L313: 
L314:     import XXX.YYY.ZZZ
L315: 
L316: 
L317: should expose `XXX.YYY.ZZZ` as a usable expression, but .moduleY is not a valid expression.
L318: ## 5.8. Special considerations for __main__cite89†¶ L319: 
L320: The cite90†`__main__` module is a special case relative to Python’s import system. As noted cite58†elsewhere , the `__main__` module is directly initialized at interpreter startup, much like cite91†`sys` and cite92†`builtins` . However, unlike those two, it doesn’t strictly qualify as a built-in module. This is because the manner in which `__main__` is initialized depends on the flags and other options with which the interpreter is invoked.
L321: ### 5.8.1. __main__.__spec__cite59†¶ L322: 
L323: Depending on how cite90†`__main__` is initialized, `__main__.__spec__` gets set appropriately or to `None`.
L324: 
L325: When Python is started with the cite93†`-m` option, `__spec__` is set to the module spec of the corresponding module or package. `__spec__` is also populated when the `__main__` module is loaded as part of executing a directory, zipfile or other cite23†`sys.path` entry.
L326: In cite94†the remaining cases `__main__.__spec__` is set to `None`, as the code used to populate the cite90†`__main__` does not correspond directly with an importable module:
L327: 
L328:   * interactive prompt
L329: 
L330:   * cite95†`-c` option
L331: 
L332:   * running from stdin
L333: 
L334:   * running directly from a source or bytecode file
L335: Note that `__main__.__spec__` is always `None` in the last case, even if the file could technically be imported directly as a module instead. Use the cite93†`-m` switch if valid module metadata is desired in cite90†`__main__` .
L336: Note also that even when `__main__` corresponds with an importable module and `__main__.__spec__` is set accordingly, they’re still considered distinct modules. This is due to the fact that blocks guarded by `if __name__ == "__main__":` checks only execute when the module is used to populate the `__main__` namespace, and not during normal import.
L337: ## 5.9. Referencescite96†¶ L338: 
L339: The import machinery has evolved considerably since Python’s early days. The original cite97†specification for packages†www.python.org is still available to read, although some details have changed since the writing of that document.
L340: 
L341: The original specification for cite10†`sys.meta_path` was cite9†PEP 302†www.python.org , with subsequent extension in cite11†PEP 420†www.python.org .
L342: cite11†PEP 420†www.python.org introduced cite20†namespace packages for Python 3.3. cite11†PEP 420†www.python.org also introduced the `find_loader()` protocol as an alternative to `find_module()`.
L343: 
L344: cite56†PEP 366†www.python.org describes the addition of the `__package__` attribute for explicit relative imports in main modules.
L345: cite98†PEP 328†www.python.org introduced absolute and explicit relative imports and initially proposed `__name__` for semantics cite56†PEP 366†www.python.org would eventually specify for `__package__`.
L346: 
L347: cite99†PEP 338†www.python.org defines executing modules as scripts.
L348: cite100†PEP 451†www.python.org adds the encapsulation of per-module import state in spec objects. It also off-loads most of the boilerplate responsibilities of loaders back onto the import machinery. These changes allow the deprecation of several APIs in the import system and also addition of new methods to finders and loaders.
